          "Now the swarm. The orchestrator splits the strategic question into three jobs. Agent Alpha handles valuation and modeling. Agent Beta handles regulatory and antitrust risk. Agent Gamma handles cybersecurity and technology infrastructure. Each writes its findings on the shared blackboard, and the orchestrator reads them together. This is the same pattern as Day 12, applied to a new case.",
          "The lab objective is the strategic acquisition of Quantum Logistics Inc. Three agents were active. The mean confidence is 0.91. The lesson sets a rule that the orchestrator needs unanimous approval and confidence of at least 0.85 before recommending action. The mean exceeds the threshold by 0.91 minus 0.85, which is 0.06. The consensus line reads boardroom proceed, accredited. Both conditions had to be met, not just one.",
          "Why both conditions? Unanimity alone could mean three agents agreeing weakly. High confidence alone could mean one agent was sure and another objected. Requiring both means every domain approved and the group is fairly sure. A student might think a high average is enough. It is not, because an average can hide one dissenting specialist. For a manager, the lesson is to ask about both agreement and strength of agreement before approving a strategic move."
