          "Read the lab. The audit covered 100,000 queries. The cache hit rate was 35.0 percent, so about 35,000 of the queries were answered without calling the model. The unit cost was 0.00341 dollars per query against a ceiling of 0.01. Multiplying, 100,000 times 0.00341 gives 341 dollars in total. If every query had cost the full ceiling, the total would have been 1,000 dollars. So the actual bill is 659 dollars lower than the ceiling would allow.",
          "Notice the relationship between the cache and the unit cost. Queries answered from the cache cost nothing, so they pull down the average. The lesson reports that caching can serve up to 40 percent of repetitive prompts, and this run achieved 35.0 percent, which sits inside that range. The verdict is FinOps budget compliant. A frequent mistake is to read the average cost as the cost of each call. Cached calls cost zero, while uncached calls cost more than the average.",
          "The lesson claims that these optimizations ensure sustainable unit economics. Treat that as a design goal that must be monitored, not a promise. Volume and prompt length change over time. That is why the engine keeps tracking rolling query volumes, token usage and blended cost, and compares them with the ceiling continuously. For a manager, the useful number to ask for each month is cost per transaction, along with the cache hit rate that helped produce it."
