          "With security defenses, red-team fuzzing, and factual evals operating smoothly, we now construct Pillar 4 of the Enterprise Control Tower: Statutory Compliance, ISO/NIST Auditing, and Cryptographic Compliance Ledgers. As established in Day 19, modern AI systems must satisfy binding legal mandates (EU AI Act), international management certifications (ISO/IEC 42001), and national risk management standards (NIST AI RMF 1.0). In an enterprise control tower, Pillar 4 monitors these dimensions continuously, translating regulatory mandates into verifiable runtime gates.",
          "Pillar 4 enforces five statutory compliance checkpoints: (1) Mandatory Risk Classification under the EU AI Act (evaluating whether new system capabilities alter risk tier classifications); (2) High-Risk Conformity Enforcement (verifying that Article 9 risk management, Article 10 data bias audits, and Article 14 human-in-the-loop controls remain active); (3) Disparate Impact Algorithmic Fairness Verification (checking that demographic approval ratios remain at least 0.80 under the Four-Fifths rule); (4) ISO 42001 Statement of Applicability Conformance; and (5) Cryptographic Ledger Chaining.",
          "Every compliance evaluation, audit finding, and runtime exception is packaged into a tamper-evident audit record and hashed using SHA-256. Each block references the cryptographic hash of the preceding block, generating an unbroken proof-of-compliance chain. This cryptographic trail provides enterprise legal counsel, board audit committees, and external regulatory authorities with tamper-evident mathematical evidence that the enterprise recorded continuous, proactive governance over its AI fleet.",
          "In this part, we implement the Pillar 4 Compliance Engine in Python. Our system verifies statutory compliance criteria, calculates demographic fairness ratios, seals audit records into a hash-chained, tamper-evident ledger, and reports compliance status to the master Control Tower.",
