          "In this grand capstone finale, we implement the complete `MasterSupplyChainControlTower` in Python. We ingest live telemetry across all four pillars, evaluate operational health gates, compute the 100/100 Global Supply Chain Excellence Score, generate a SHA-256 verification token, and confer the CGSCLD course credential.",
          "Now we assemble the four pillars into one score. Each pillar contributes 25 points, and the lab shows all four earning exactly that, with a status of pass. Add them up and you get 100 out of a maximum of 100. The tower then authorizes autonomous operations and awards the course credential, and it prints the start of a verification token, which begins a4e0f8db4bb85a77bd61b190.",
          "Look at how the composite is built. It is a plain sum of four pass or fail results, each worth the same. That makes it easy to read, but also easy to misread. A score of 100 does not mean the supply chain is perfect. It means that each pillar's specific checks, in this lab, with these inputs, were met. The inputs in the lab were chosen to pass. In a real company the interesting moments are when a pillar earns 0 and the tower tells you where to look.",
          "What is the commercial value the quiz asks about? Think of it as evidence. A tower that shows all four areas healthy, with numbers a board can inspect, gives leaders a reason to allow more automation, and it gives the company a common language for talking about its supply chain. The credential named in the lesson is a course credential, as the lesson itself says, and not an industry-recognized certification. Its value is as a record of what you have practiced.",
          "The verification token is worth a word. It is a fixed-length fingerprint made from the results, so that anyone can later check that the report was not changed. It does not prove the underlying data was true. It only shows that the report has not been altered since it was created. That is a useful control, but it sits on top of honest inputs.",
          "To recap Milestone 4, we forecast demand and tamed the bullwhip, sized and pooled stock, routed vehicles and cut carbon, scored suppliers and matched invoices, and then scored them all together. The next milestone moves from running an operation to leading change in an organization. In Day 26, we look at how a company organizes itself around AI and builds a center of excellence."
