        "part": 2,
        "title": "Pillar 2: Medallion Lakehouse Data Lineage, Semantic Catalog & Cryptographic Truth",
        "say": [
          "An autonomous cognitive enterprise cannot function on corrupted, stale, or hallucinated data. If underlying data pipelines inject faulty schemas or untracked state mutations into production models, the entire multi-agent swarm will make flawed business decisions at machine speed.",
          "In Part 2, we operationalize Pillar 2: Medallion Lakehouse Data Lineage, Semantic Catalog & Cryptographic Truth. This subsystem governs the end-to-end data refinery: ingesting raw operational telemetry into the Bronze layer, executing deterministic deduplication and schema validation in the Silver layer, and computing certified business metrics in the Gold layer.",
          "Crucially, Pillar 2 enforces cryptographic SHA-256 data provenance and Great Expectations automated data quality contracts. Every dataset referenced by an autonomous agent carries an immutable cryptographic digest and verifiable lineage graph. If an upstream dataset suffers null spikes, distribution drift, or unauthorized schema mutations, Pillar 2 blocks downstream model inference instantly.",
          "In this second capstone segment, we implement the Pillar 2 Medallion Lakehouse Data Integrity Engine in Python. We validate Bronze-to-Silver-to-Gold progression, calculate Great Expectations data quality passage rates, verify cryptographic hash lineage, and compute the Pillar 2 Data Integrity Score (maximum 25.0 points).",
          "Agents are only as good as the information they read. If the data is stale, duplicated or quietly altered, the agents will act on it quickly and confidently. That is why the second pillar is about data. The lesson describes a refinery with three stages, called bronze, silver and gold, and then adds a way to prove that the data has not been tampered with.",
          "Bronze holds the raw material, as it arrived. Silver is where it is cleaned: duplicates are removed and the structure is checked. Gold holds the finished business figures that people and agents actually use. At every stage, the system runs a list of quality checks. In the lab, bronze has 10 checks, silver has 15 and gold has 12. All of them pass, so 37 out of 37 are satisfied and the data quality is 100.0 percent.",
          "Next comes the fingerprint. For each layer, the system computes a digest with a method called SHA-256. A digest is a short code produced from the data. If even one character of the data changes, the code changes completely. The system stores the codes along with the lineage, which is the record of where each dataset came from. In the lab, the lineage is marked verified when quality is at least 99 percent, and the cryptographic lineage flag is true.",
          "That explains the quiz. Before gold data reaches the agents, the company wants proof that it is the same data that passed the checks, and not something altered on the way. With the fingerprint, a mismatch is caught at once and the downstream inference is blocked. Without it, a silent change could flow into decisions at machine speed. The fingerprint does not prove the data is correct in the first place. It proves the data is unchanged since it was validated.",
          "A common misunderstanding is that a high quality score means the data is true. It means the data passed the defined checks. If the checks are poor, the score is meaningless. A good manager asks who wrote the checks and how often they are updated. With the data layer in place, we can turn to protecting the whole system from attack.",
          "Here is a short worked picture of the quality score. Suppose a single check in the silver layer had failed. Then 36 of the 37 checks would pass, which is about 97.3 percent. That is below the 99 percent line the lab uses for verified lineage, so the pillar would not award its full points and downstream inference would be blocked until the cause was fixed. This shows how strict the gate is: a tiny fraction of failed checks is enough to stop the agents from using the data."
        ],
        "example": "Worked example: Bronze (10/10 checks), Silver (15/15) and Gold (12/12) all pass, so data quality is 100.0%; a SHA-256 digest is computed for each layer's payload, lineage is marked verified (quality >= 99%) and the pillar scores 25.0.",
        "code": "import hashlib\nimport json\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, asdict\nfrom typing import List, Dict\n\n@dataclass\nclass MedallionPartitionAudit:\n    layer: str  # 'BRONZE', 'SILVER', 'GOLD'\n    record_count: int\n    quality_checks_passed: int\n    total_quality_checks: int\n    payload_raw_data: str\n\n@dataclass\nclass Pillar2Telemetry:\n    pillar_id: str = 'Pillar_2_Medallion_Data_Integrity'\n    layers_validated: int = 0\n    overall_data_quality_pct: float = 0.0\n    cryptographic_lineage_verified: bool = False\n    pillar_score: float = 0.0  # Max 25.0 pts\n\nclass Pillar2DataIntegrityEngine:\n    def evaluate_lakehouse_integrity(self, partitions: List[MedallionPartitionAudit]) -> Pillar2Telemetry:\n        if not partitions:\n            return Pillar2Telemetry()\n            \n        total_checks = sum(p.total_quality_checks for p in partitions)\n        passed_checks = sum(p.quality_checks_passed for p in partitions)\n        quality_pct = (passed_checks / total_checks) * 100.0 if total_checks > 0 else 0.0\n        \n        # Cryptographic chain verification\n        hashes = []\n        for p in partitions:\n            h = hashlib.sha256(p.payload_raw_data.encode('utf-8')).hexdigest()\n            hashes.append(h)\n            \n        lineage_verified = len(hashes) == len(partitions) and quality_pct >= 99.0\n        score = 25.0 if lineage_verified else round((quality_pct / 100.0) * 20.0, 1)\n        \n        return Pillar2Telemetry(\n            layers_validated=len(partitions),\n            overall_data_quality_pct=round(quality_pct, 2),\n            cryptographic_lineage_verified=lineage_verified,\n            pillar_score=score\n        )\n\n# Test execution\nmedallion_layers = [\n    MedallionPartitionAudit('BRONZE', 100000, 10, 10, 'raw_iot_telemetry_batch_4091'),\n    MedallionPartitionAudit('SILVER', 98500, 15, 15, 'cleaned_deduped_orders_silver_4091'),\n    MedallionPartitionAudit('GOLD', 25000, 12, 12, 'aggregated_enterprise_kpis_gold_4091')\n]\nengine2 = Pillar2DataIntegrityEngine()\ntelem2 = engine2.evaluate_lakehouse_integrity(medallion_layers)\nprint(json.dumps(asdict(telem2), indent=2))\n",
        "output": "{\n  \"pillar_id\": \"Pillar_2_Medallion_Data_Integrity\",\n  \"layers_validated\": 3,\n  \"overall_data_quality_pct\": 100.0,\n  \"cryptographic_lineage_verified\": true,\n  \"pillar_score\": 25.0\n}",
